    "response": "**Structured Response on Confirmatory Genetic Testing for **Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator (CFTR) Mutations (CTX)**\n\n### **1. Background**\nCystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the *CFTR* gene (chromosome 7q31), encoding the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator protein. Biochemical suspicion of CF is often confirmed via **sweat chloride testing** (performed according to the **NBS-ACG/SBCT guidelines**), which measures elevated chloride concentrations (>60 mmol/L) in sweat. However, if sweat testing is inconclusive or if clinical suspicion remains high despite normal results, **genetic testing** serves as the definitive confirmatory method.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Confirmatory Genetic Testing Approaches**\n#### **A. Direct Sequencing of the CFTR Gene (Whole-Gene Analysis)**\n- **Method**: High-throughput sequencing (e.g., Sanger sequencing, next-generation sequencing [NGS]) of all 27 exons and intronic regions of the *CFTR* gene.\n- **Key Features**:\n  - Detects **all known pathogenic variants**, including:\n    - **Class I\u2013V mutations** (e.g., nonsense, frameshift, splice-site, missense, or regulatory variants).\n    - **Compound heterozygosity** (two distinct pathogenic variants in trans).\n  - **Limitations**:\n    - May miss **de novo variants** or **large deletions/duplications** (unless targeted by multiplex PCR or MLPA).\n    - Requires **family history** to distinguish inherited vs. de novo mutations.\n\n#### **B. Multiplex Ligation-Dependent Amplification (MLPA)**\n- **Method**: A targeted genetic test that detects **small deletions/duplications** (e.g., \u0394F508, 1892+1G\u2192A, 1717-1G\u2192A) using fluorescent probes.\n- **Key Features**:\n  - Highly sensitive for **class III (miscoding) and IV (reduced function) mutations**.\n  - Useful for **families with known CFTR variants** or when whole-exome sequencing (WES) is unavailable.\n\n#### **C. Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) Panel Testing**\n- **Method**: Sequences multiple genes associated with **pseudomonas aeruginosa, *F508del*, or other CFTR-related disorders** (e.g., *ABCA3*, *G5E*, *ABCD1*).\n- **Key Features**:\n  - Identifies **co-occurring genetic variants** (e.g., CFTR + *ABCA3* mutations in neonatal respiratory failure).\n  - Useful in **complex cases** where CFTR alone is insufficient.\n\n#### **D. Chromosomal Microarray (CMA) for Large Deletions**\n- **Method**: Array-based comparative genomic hybridization (aCGH) to detect **copy-number variations (CNVs)** in the *CFTR* locus.\n- **Key Features**:\n  - Detects **large deletions/duplications** (e.g., 7q31.2 deletion syndrome).\n  - Critical for **families with recurrent miscarriages or unexplained CF-like phenotypes**.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Clinical Workflow for Confirmatory Testing**\n1. **Sweat Chloride Test (Primary Screening)**\n   - **Threshold**: \u226560 mmol/L (confirms CF).\n   - **Interpretation**:\n     - **\u226560 mmol/L**: Likely CF (next step: genetic testing).\n     - **45\u201359 mmol/L**: Borderline (repeat testing or genetic testing).\n     - **<45 mmol/L**: Unlikely CF (consider other diagnoses).\n\n2. **Genetic Testing After Biochemical Suspicion**\n   - **If sweat test is equivocal (45\u201359 mmol/L)**:\n     - **Family history**: If parents are carriers, offspring have a **25% risk** of CF.\n     - **Genetic testing** (whole-exome sequencing or targeted CFTR analysis) is recommended.\n   - **If sweat test is normal but clinical suspicion remains high** (e.g., neonatal respiratory failure, pancreatic insufficiency):\n     - **Expand testing** to include:\n       - **MLPA** (for deletions/duplications).\n       - **NGS panel** (for co-occurring variants).\n       - **CMA** (if large genomic rearrangements are suspected).\n\n3. **Diagnostic Criteria for CF Confirmation**\n   - **Two pathogenic variants** in trans (*CFTR* gene) are required for a definitive diagnosis.\n   - **Exceptions**:\n     - **Neonatal respiratory failure** (e.g., meconium ileus, CFTR + *ABCA3* mutations).\n     - **Pancreatic insufficiency** (e.g., \u0394F508 + another CFTR variant).\n\n---\n\n### **4. Key Considerations**\n- **Ethical and Clinical Context**:\n  - **Informed consent** is mandatory, especially for **de novo mutations** or **recessive inheritance**.\n  - **Genetic counseling** is essential to interpret results in the context of family history.\n- **Limitations of Genetic Testing**:\n  - **False positives** (e.g., benign variants in CFTR).\n  - **False negatives** (e.g., de novo variants, large rearrangements).\n- **Alternative Diagnoses**:\n  - If CFTR mutations are absent, consider:\n    - **Other CFTR-related disorders** (e.g., *ABCA3* mutations in neonatal respiratory failure).\n    - **Metabolic disorders** (e.g., primary ciliary dyskinesia, *DNAH5* mutations).\n\n---\n### **5. Recommended Testing Algorithm**\n| **Scenario**                          | **Recommended Test**                          |\n|---------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n| Sweat chloride \u226560 mmol/L             | Whole-exome sequencing (WES) or targeted CFTR analysis |\n| Sweat chloride 45\u201359 mmol/L           | Genetic testing (WES, MLPA, or NGS panel)    |\n| Neonatal respiratory failure           | CFTR + *ABCA3* NGS panel                     |\n| Large genomic rearrangements suspected | Chromosomal microarray (CMA)                 |\n\n---\n### **6. References (for Further Reading)**\n- **NBS-ACG/SBCT Guidelines** (2023): [https://www.cfsgenetics.org](https://www.cfsgenetics.org)\n- **American College of Medical Genetics (ACMG)** Standards for CFTR Testing.\n- **Genetic Testing Registry (GTR)**: [https://www.genetests.org](https://www.genetests.org)\n\n---\n### **Final Note**\nIf the user requires **specific variant analysis** (e.g., \u0394F508, G551D, 1712G>A), please provide the **family history** and **clinical pres