    "response": "The **Celiac Disease (CD)** or **Gluten Sensitivity (GS)**, often referred to as **CTX** (for **Celiac Toxicity** or **Celiac Disease Testing**), involves a complex immunological and biochemical cascade triggered by gluten ingestion. Diagnosis relies on a combination of serological, genetic, and endoscopic tests, with the **most reliable screening test** being the **serological panel** (IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase [TTG-IgA] and/or anti-endomysium antibodies [EMA-IgA]). Below is a structured, evidence-based overview of diagnostic approaches:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Serological Tests (Screening & Confirmatory)**\nSerological markers are the **primary screening tools** for CD, though they must be interpreted in the context of clinical symptoms, genetic testing, and endoscopy.\n\n#### **A. Tissue Transglutaminase-IgA (TTG-IgA)**\n- **Mechanism**: TTG-IgA antibodies recognize deamidated gluten peptides (via transglutaminase-mediated modification), reflecting gluten-induced immune activation.\n- **Sensitivity/Specificity**:\n  - **Sensitivity**: ~90\u201395% in symptomatic patients (higher in children).\n  - **Specificity**: ~95\u201398% when positive (low false positives in non-celiac gluten sensitivity or other autoimmune diseases).\n- **Limitations**:\n  - **False negatives** in IgA-deficient patients (replaced by **deamidated gliadin peptide [dGP]-IgA**).\n  - **Cross-reactivity** with other autoimmune conditions (e.g., type 1 diabetes, dermatitis herpetiformis).\n- **Best Practice**: First-line test due to high specificity and cost-effectiveness.\n\n#### **B. Endomysium Antibodies (EMA-IgA)**\n- **Mechanism**: Detects antibodies binding to endomysium (a component of muscle fibers), a marker of gluten-induced inflammation.\n- **Sensitivity/Specificity**:\n  - **Sensitivity**: ~80\u201390% (lower than TTG-IgA).\n  - **Specificity**: ~95%.\n- **Limitations**:\n  - Less sensitive than TTG-IgA; often used as a confirmatory test if TTG is equivocal.\n  - *