fic)**\n#### **A. IgA Deficiency**\n- **Mechanism**: ~10% of CD patients are IgA-deficient. Testing for **IgA levels** is critical before serology.\n- **Test**: **IgA quantification** (normal range: 0.7\u20134.0 g/L).\n\n#### **B. Other Autoimmune Markers**\n- **ANA (Antinuclear Antibodies)**: Elevated in ~30% of CD patients (due to shared autoantigens).\n- **ASMA (Anti-Smooth Muscle Antibodies)**: Rarely used but may indicate overlap with autoimmune hepatitis.\n\n#### **C. Gluten Challenge (Diagnostic Challenge)**\n- **Mechanism**: After 6+ months on gluten-free diet (GFD), patients with CD may develop:\n  - **Villous atrophy** (Marsh III) on biopsy.\n  - **Serological relapse** (TTG/EMA positivity).\n- **Use Case**: Rarely performed due to ethical concerns and lack of standardization.\n\n#### **D. Exclusive Enteral Nutrition (EEN) Trial**\n- **Mechanism**: Used in **pediatric CD diagnosis** (e.g., in children with suspected CD but negative serology).\n- **Procedure**: 6\u201312 weeks of gluten-free enteral nutrition followed by serology/biopsy.\n\n---\n### **5. Most Reliable Screening Test: TTG-IgA**\n| **Test**               | **Sensitivity** | **Specificity** | **First-Line?** | **Notes**                          |\n|------------------------|-----------------|-----------------|-----------------|------------------------------------|\n| **TTG-IgA**            | 90\u201395%          | 95\u201398%          | **Yes**         | Gold standard for screening.        |\n| **EMA-IgA**            | 80\u201390%          | 95%             | No              | Confirmatory if TTG is equivocal. |\n| **dGP-IgA**            | 80\u201390%          | 95%             | Yes (IgA-deficient) | Replaces TTG in IgA-deficient patients. |\n| **AGA-IgG**            | 50\u201370%          | 80\u201390%          | No              | Not recommended.                   |\n\n---\n### **6. Diagnostic Algorithm (Evidence-Based)**\n1. **Screening**:\n   - **First-line**: **TTG-IgA** (if normal, rule 