l or generalized seizures** (often refractory to antiepileptic drugs).\n- **Psychiatric Symptoms**:\n  - **Depression**, **anxiety**, or **psychosis** (due to cortical demyelination).\n\n#### **C. Systemic Manifestations (Non-Neurological)**\n- **Adrenal Insufficiency**:\n  - **Addison\u2019s disease** (hypocortisolism) due to adrenal cortex demyelination.\n  - **Symptoms**: Fatigue, weight loss, hyperpigmentation, hypotension.\n- **Renal Involvement**:\n  - **Proximal renal tubular acidosis** (due to VLCFA accumulation in proximal tubules).\n\n---\n### **2. Brain MRI Findings in Untreated CXLD**\nBrain MRI is **pathognomonic** for CXLD, showing **inflammatory demyelination** with distinct patterns:\n\n#### **A. Classic \"Butterfly\" Lesions**\n- **Bilateral symmetric demyelination** in the **periventricular white matter**, sparing the **corpus callosum** (unlike other leukodystrophies).\n- **Enhancement pattern**:\n  - **T1-weighted post-contrast MRI** shows **ring-enhancing lesions** (due to breakdown of the blood-brain barrier).\n  - **T2/FLAIR images** reveal **hyperintense white matter plaques** (demyelination).\n\n#### **B. Additional Findings**\n- **Optic Nerve Involvement**:\n  - **Optic neuritis** (enlarged, hyperintense optic nerves on MRI).\n  - **Optic atrophy** (atrophy of optic chiasm/ganglion cells).\n- **Brainstem and Cerebellar Demyelination**:\n  - **Bilateral brainstem lesions** (especially in the **pons and midbrain**).\n  - **Cerebellar atrophy** and **white matter changes** (e.g., **diffuse hyperintensities** on FLAIR).\n- **Cortical Involvement**:\n  - **Cortical demyelination** (less common but seen in advanced disease).\n- **Atrophy**:\n  - **Global cerebral atrophy** (due to chronic demyelination).\n\n#### **C. Special Imaging Techniques**\n- **Diffusion-Weighted Imaging (DWI)**:\n  - Shows **restricted diffusion** in active demyelinating lesions.\n- **Susceptibility Weighted Imaging (SWI)**:\n  - Reveals **microhemorrhages** or **calcifications** (in advanced disease).\n- **MR Spectroscopy (MRS)**:\n  - **Increased VLCFA peaks** (e.g., C26:0) and **reduced N-acetylaspartate (NAA)** (neuronal loss).\n\n---\n### **3. Differential Diagnosis**\nDifferential considerations include:\n- **Metachromatic Leukodystrophy (MLD)**:\n  - Similar MRI patterns but **no adrenal involvement**.\n- **Krabbe Disease**:\n  - **Global white matter demyelination** (unlike CXLD\u2019s periventricular sparing).\n- **Adrenomyeloneuropathy (AMN)**:\n  - **Adult-onset** form of X-ALD with **spinal cord involvement** (e.g., **spinal cord atrophy**).\n- **Inflammatory Demyelinating Diseases** (e.g., MS):\n  - **No VLCFA accumulation** or **adrenal involvement**.\n\n---\n### **4. Prognosis and Treatment**\n- **Untreated CXLD is fatal** within **5\u201310 years**, typically due to:\n  - **Respiratory failure** (from cerebellar/bulbar involvement).\n  - **Sepsis** (due to adrenal insufficiency).\n- **Emerging Therapies**:\n  - **Mitochondrial gene therapy** (e.g., **ABCD1 gene therapy trials**).\n  - **Hemochromatosis gene therapy** (experimental).\n  - **Lysosomal enzyme replacement** (e.g., **ALADOCEN** in clinical trials).\n  - **Early MRI-guided interventions** (e.g., **adrenal steroid replacement**, **fat-soluble vitamin therapy**).\n\n---\n### **Key Takeaways**\n| **Feature**               | **Neurological Manifestations**                          | **MRI Findings**                                  |\n|---------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|\n| **Early Signs**           | Optic atrophy, visual disturbances, gait ataxia        | Bilateral periventricular ring-enhancing lesions    |\n| **Progressive Features**  | Cognitive decline, seizures, autonomic failure         | Optic nerve involvement, brainstem demyelination  |\n| **Advanced Stages**      | Cerebellar ataxia, cranial nerve palsies               | Cortical demyelination, atrop