ased N-acetylaspartate (NAA)** (marker of neuronal loss).\n- **Lipid accumulation** (e.g., cholestanol peaks).\n- **Elevated lactate** (indicative of mitochondrial dysfunction).\n\n---\n### **Key Diagnostic Considerations**\n1. **MRI as a Diagnostic Tool**:\n   - **Dentate nuclei hyperintensity** (T2/FLAIR) is a **strong but nonspecific** indicator.\n   - **Absence of MRI abnormalities does not exclude CTX** (e.g., early-stage disease or atypical presentation).\n   - **Spinal MRI** may reveal lesions in **24% of cases**, often with a **milder clinical course** compared to cerebral CTX.\n\n2. **Biochemical and Genetic Confirmation**:\n   - **Increased plasma cholestanol** (diagnostic but not definitive).\n   - **Genetic testing (CYP27A1 mutations)** is the **gold standard** for diagnosis.\n\n3. **Clinical Correlation**:\n   - MRI findings may **precede neurological symptoms** (e.g., cerebellar atrophy in asymptomatic patients).\n   - **EEG findings** (e.g., diffuse slow waves) may complement imaging.\n\n---\n### **Prognostic Implications**\n- **Progressive neurodegeneration** leads to **intellectual decline, motor disability, and autonomic dysfunction**.\n- **Early diagnosis and treatment** (e.g., **choleretic therapy**) may slow disease progression but does not reverse established damage.\n\n---\n**References**:\n- PMC7939202, PMC4833925, PMC12196618, PMC7342084, PMC10701218.",
