osition in synovium).\n  - **Growth retardation** (due to malabsorption or metabolic derangements).\n\n#### **3. Biochemical and Genetic Clues**\n- **Lipid profile abnormalities**:\n  - **Elevated serum TGs** (>500 mg/dL) and **cholesteryl ester accumulation** (due to defective ABCA1-mediated lipid efflux).\n  - **Low HDL cholesterol** (due to impaired ABCA1-mediated cholesterol efflux).\n- **Genetic testing**:\n  - **Sanger sequencing or NGS** for *ABCA1* mutations (e.g., c.1036C>T [p.Arg346Trp], c.1620G>A [p.Glu540Lys]).\n  - **Family history**: Autosomal recessive inheritance in consanguineous families.\n\n#### **4. Neurological Precursors (If Present Before Full Manifestation)**\n- **Early signs of CNS involvement** (if neurological disability is imminent):\n  - **Delayed motor milestones** (e.g., delayed sitting, crawling, or walking).\n  - **Seizures** (e.g., focal or generalized, often refractory to standard antiepileptics).\n  - **Hypotonia** or **spasticity** (due to lipid deposition in the basal ganglia or cerebellum).\n  - **Progressive cognitive decline** (e.g., regression in developmental milestones).\n\n---\n\n### **Differential Diagnosis Considerations**\nCTX must be distinguished from other lipid storage disorders with similar early features:\n| **Disorder**               | **Key Features**                                                                 | **ABCA1-Specific Clues**                     |\n|---------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n| **Tangier Disease**        | **Tonsillar hypertrophy**, **orange-colored tonsils**, **neurological decline**   | Less systemic xanthomas, **no ocular involvement** |\n| **Neurovisceral Lipidosis** (e.g., **GM1 Gangliosidosis**) | **Neurological regression**, **hepatosplenomegaly** | **No cutaneous xanthomas**, different genetic basis |\n| **Cholesteryl Ester Storage Disease (CESD)** | **Hepatos