 - **Other lysosomal storage diseases** (e.g., Gaucher disease, Niemann-Pick disease) may present with generalized lipid storage.\n\n#### **C. Imaging and Histopathology**\n- **Ultrasound:** Shows hypoechoic or echogenic nodules in tendons.\n- **MRI:** May reveal fat signal intensity in affected tendons.\n- **Histopathology:** Characterized by **foam cells, lipid droplets, and collagen disruption** (unlike CTX, which shows generalized lysosomal storage).\n- **Biopsy:** If CTX is suspected, **systemic tissue biopsy** (e.g., subcutaneous fat) may show **lipid accumulation in lysosomes** (e.g., vacuoles in fibroblasts).\n\n#### **D. Differential Diagnosis Summary**\n| Feature                | **Tendon Xanthomas**                          | **Connective Tissue Xanthomas (CTX)**          |\n|------------------------|---------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n| **Location**           | Localized to tendons (Achilles, extensor tendons) | Generalized (tendons, joints, subcutaneous fat) |\n| **Lipid profile**      | Elevated LDL, triglycerides (hyperlipidemia) | Normal or mildly elevated lipids             |\n| **Genetics**           | FH (*LDLR*, *APOB*), familial hyperlipidemia | *LIPA* mutation (LALD), lysosomal storage diseases |\n| **Histopathology**     | Foam cells, lipid droplets in tendons         | Lysosomal lipid accumulation (vacuoles)      |\n| **Systemic involvement** | Rare (unless severe hyperlipidemia)          | Yes (joint pain, muscle weakness, growth retardation) |\n\n---\n\n### **3. Key Distinguishing Features Between Tendon Xanthomas and CTX**\n- **Tendon xanthomas** are **secondary to systemic hyperlipidemia** (e.g., familial hypercholesterolemia) and do not involve generalized lysosomal storage.\n- **CTX (e.g., LALD)** is a **primary lysosomal storage disorder** with **generalized lipid accumulation** in multiple tissues, including tendons, joints, and subcutaneous fat.\n- **Lipid profile** is more in