OC 2, DOC 9).\n  - **Differential Considerations**: Rule out other conditions with tendon xanthomas (e.g., familial hypercholesterolemia, sitosterolemia), which lack neurological manifestations (DOC 2).\n\n#### **MRI**\n- **Characteristic Findings**:\n  - **Central Nervous System (CNS)**:\n    - Bilateral T2-weighted hyperintensities in the **dentate nuclei** (hallmark of CTX) extending to cerebellar white matter and cerebral hemispheres (DOC 2, DOC 9).\n    - Subcortical white matter hyperintensities, periventricular atrophy, or fronto-parietal atrophy (DOC 9).\n  - **Tendons**:\n    - Achilles tendon: Thickened, striated hypointensity on T1 and isointensity on T2 due to lipid deposition (DOC 9).\n    - Other tendons (e.g., extensor tendons of the elbow) may also show similar changes (DOC 4).\n\n#### **Additional Modalities**\n- **CT Scans**: Rule out bony involvement (e.g., xanthomatous bone lesions) or lung infiltrates (DOC 4).\n- **Palpebral Xanthelasma**: Visible as yellowish plaques on the eyelids (DOC 4).\n\n---\n\n### **3. Biochemical Testing**\n#### **Core Tests**\n- **Serum Cholestanol Measurement**:\n  - **Gold Standard**: Elevated cholestanol (>5.0 mg/L) strongly supports CTX (DOC 8, DOC 9).\n  - **Limitations**: Not obligatory if clinical/radiological findings are highly suggestive (DOC 5).\n\n- **Bile Acid Profile**:\n  - Decreased conjugated bile acids (e.g., CDCA) and increased bile alcohols/glycoconjugates (e.g., coprostanol) (DOC 8).\n\n#### **Additional Sterols**\n- **Lipid Profile**: Elevated 7-dehydrocholesterol, lathosterol, or lanosterol (DOC 8).\n\n#### **Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF) Analysis**\n- **Key Abnormalities**:\n  - Increased cholestanol and apolipoprotein B concentrations (DOC 8).\n\n---\n\n### **4. Genetic Testing**\n#### **Gold Standard**\n- **CYP27A1 Gene Analysis**:\n  - **Pathogenic Mutations**: Confirms CTX diagnosis (DOC 6, DOC 8).\n  - **Variants of Uncertain Significance (VUS)**: Requires comprehensive biochemical/clinical correlation (DOC 8).\n\n#### **Limitations**\n- **Accessibility**: Genetic testing may not always be available (DOC 5).\n- **Alternative Approach**: Biochemical profiling (e.g., cholestanol levels) can guide clinical suspicion (DOC 5).\n\n---\n\n### **5. Differential Diagnosis**\n| **Condition**               | **Key Features**                                                                 |\n|-----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Familial Hypercholesterolemia** | Elevated LDL, tendon xanthomas (no neurological/cataract manifestations).          |\n| **Sitosterolemia**          | Elevated plant sterols (e.g., sitosterol), xanthomas (no CTX-specific features).  |\n| **Statin-Induced Xanthomas** | Tendon xanthomas post-statin therapy; no cholestanol elevation.                  |\n| **Other Metabolic Disorders** | e.g., Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome (elevated 7-dehydrocholesterol but no CTX-specific features). |\n\n---\n\n### **6. Management and Follow-Up**\n#### **Therapeutic Approaches**\n- **Bile Acid Therapy**:\n  - **Chenodeoxycholic Acid (CDCA)**: Reduces cholestanol and bile alcohol accumulation (DOC 10).\n  - **Effectiveness**: Improves symptoms (e.g., diarrhea, neurological signs) but may not reverse neurological damage (DOC 10).\n- **Surgical Intervention**:\n  - **Achilles Tendon Xanthoma Excision**: Rarely effective due to integration with tendon fibers (DOC 10).\n  - **Alternative**: Vascularized flap reconstruction for functional outcomes (DOC 10).\n- **Statin Therapy**: May exacerbate or improve xanthomas; individualize based on patient response (DOC 10).\n\n#### **Monitoring**\n- **Biochemical**: Regular cholestanol and bile acid levels.\n- **Neurological**: Neurological decline or progression warrants early intervention.\n- **Imaging**: Follow-up MRI/ultrasound to monitor tendon/neurological changes.\n\n---\n\n### **Summary Algorithm for Evaluation**\n1. **Clinical**: Confirm tendon xanthomas + systemic features (e.g., cataracts, family history).\n2. **Biochemical**: Measure serum cholestanol; consider bile acid profile.\n3. **Imaging**:\n   - **Ultrasound**: Tendon xanthomas.\n   - **MRI**: CNS hyperintensities (dentate nuclei) + tendon changes.\n4. **Genetic**: CYP27A1 testing if available.\n5. **Exclude Alternatives**: Rule out familial hypercholesterolemia, sitosterolemia, etc.\n6. **Therapeutic**: Initiate bile acid therapy or refer for genetic counseling/surgical evaluation.\n\n---\n**Key Takeaway**: Tendon xanthomas in CTX require a **multidisciplinary approach** combining clinical correlation, imaging (MRI/ultrasound), biochemical testing (cholestanol/bile acids), and genetic analysis. Early diagnosis is critical for guiding personalized therapy and preventing neurological progression.",
