 3 months** (DOC 5, DOC 7, DOC 8).\n  - **Action if elevated**: Increase CDCA dose to **1,000 mg/day** if biochemical markers remain abnormal after 3 months (DOC 5).\n\n#### **B. Clinical Monitoring**\n- **Neurological progression**:\n  - **Early intervention** is critical. Patients treated **before age 24** show **complete symptom resolution** (DOC 8), whereas those treated later (e.g., >25 years) exhibit **resistance to parkinsonism** (DOC 8).\n  - **Symptom tracking**: Neurological decline (e.g., tremor, gait instability, cognitive decline) warrants **dose escalation or adjunctive therapy** (e.g., CA, if tolerated).\n- **Tendon xanthomas/cataracts**:\n  - CDCA **does not improve tendon xanthomas or cataracts** (DOC 2). These are **irreversible** but may stabilize with long-term therapy.\n\n#### **C. Special Populations**\n- **Pediatric patients**:\n  - **Dose**: 5 mg/kg/day (DOC 5). Monitor closely for **growth delays** (common in CTX) and **hepatotoxicity**.\n  - **Early diagnosis**: Critical to prevent irreversible neurological damage (DOC 8).\n- **Adolescents**:\n  - **Symptom onset** often occurs in late adolescence/early adulthood. **Delay in diagnosis** correlates with **poor response** (DOC 8).\n\n---\n\n### **3. Safety Monitoring**\n#### **A. Hepatotoxicity**\n- **Transient transaminase elevations** are common in the first **3\u20136 months** of therapy (DOC 5, DOC 10).\n  - **Monitor**: **AST/ALT every 2 weeks** initially, then **every 3 months**.\n  - **Action if elevated**:\n    - **Temporarily reduce dose** (e.g., 500 mg/day for 1\u20132 weeks).\n    - **Reassess biochemical markers** before resuming full dose.\n    - **Consider alternative therapy** (e.g., CA) if hepatotoxicity persists (DOC 2).\n- **Persistent elevated transaminases** (>3\u00d7 ULN) or **jaundice** \u2192 **discontinue CDCA** and consult hepatology.\n\n#### **B. Other Adverse Effects**\n- **Gastrointestinal**:\n  - Diarrhea, nausea, or abdominal pain may occur. Adjust dose or switch