ntestinal**: Rare but may include **nausea, diarrhea** (often transient).\n- **Neurological**: CDCA does **not** improve **tendon xanthomas** or **cataracts**, but may **stabilize** neurological symptoms (e.g., polyneuropathy, cerebellar ataxia) if started **early**.\n- **Infections**: Low risk; **CTX + traditional Chinese medicine** studies reported **few infections** (likely due to underlying immune dysregulation).\n- **Cardiovascular**: **Heart failure** in elderly patients with **pre-existing conditions** (not CDCA-specific).\n\n#### **C. Biomarker Monitoring**\n- **Cholestanol**: **Target <5.0 mg/L** (63% of patients achieve this with CDCA).\n- **Urine bile alcohols**: Correlates with **bile acid synthesis suppression**; useful for **compliance assessment**.\n- **FXR pathway activity**: Indirectly assessed via **cholestanol levels** (CDCA inhibits CYP7A1 via FXR).\n\n#### **D. Long-Term Follow-Up**\n- **Frequency**: **Every 3\u20136 months** for:\n  - **Biochemical markers** (cholestanol, transaminases).\n  - **Neurological status** (e.g., ambulation, cognitive function).\n  - **Adverse events** (e.g., hepatotoxicity, GI symptoms).\n- **Alternative therapy evaluation**: If **CDCA fails to stabilize biomarkers** or causes **severe side effects**, consider **cholic acid (CA)** or **other bile acid derivatives** (e.g., ursodeoxycholic acid).\n\n---\n\n### **4. Alternative Therapies and Backup Plans**\n- **Cholic Acid (CA)**: May be used as a **second-line option** if CDCA is poorly tolerated or ineffective. **80% of patients** show clinical improvement with CA.\n- **Cholestyramine**: Not recommended as monotherapy; may interfere with CDCA absorption.\n- **FXR agonists**: Experimental therapies (e.g., obeticholic acid) are being explored but not yet standard.\n\n---\n\n### **5. Practical Recommendations for Clinicians**\n| **Domain**          | **Action**                                                                 |\n|---------------------|---------------------