e Testing**\n#### **A. Who Should Undergo Testing?**\n| **Group**               | **Rationale**                                                                 |\n|-------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Siblings of the index case** | Highest risk of being homozygous (if parents are carriers).                   |\n| **Parents of the index case** | Identify carriers (who may be asymptomatic but could pass the disease).       |\n| **Consanguineous relatives** | Increased likelihood of homozygous pathogenic variants.                     |\n| **Unaffected relatives with multisystemic features** | Rule out other genetic disorders (e.g., Niemann-Pick C) or early-onset CTX. |\n\n#### **B. Testing Strategies**\n1. **Gene-Targeted Testing (Single-Gene or Multigene Panel)**\n   - **Primary target**: **CYP27A1** (DOCS 7, 10).\n   - **Alternative targets**: If CYP27A1 is not identified, consider **multigene panels** (e.g., lipid storage disorders) to cover rare variants.\n2. **Exome/Whole Genome Sequencing (WGS)**\n   - Useful for **complex cases** with atypical presentations or unknown genetic causes.\n3. **Prenatal Testing**\n   - If parents are carriers, **amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling (CVS)** can confirm fetal carrier status or CTX risk.\n\n#### **C. Clinical Follow-Up**\n- **Asymptomatic carriers**: Regular surveillance for early-onset features (e.g., cataracts, diarrhea).\n- **Affected relatives**: Early intervention with **CDCA therapy** (DOCS 6) to mitigate disease progression.\n- **Neurological monitoring**: EEG, MRI, and cognitive assessments to detect early neurological decline.\n\n---\n\n### **4. Key Challenges and Considerations**\n- **Phenotypic Variability**: While cascade testing improves diagnostic accuracy, the **high intra-familial variability** (DOCS 2\u20133) requires **individualized risk assessment** rather than a one-size-fits-all approach.\n- **Psychosocial Impact**: Cascade testing may reveal carrier status in unaffected relatives, necessitating **genetic counseling** to address emotional and reproductive concerns.\n- **Therapeutic Advances**: Emerging data on **CDCA and coenzyme Q10** (DOCS 4) highlight the need for **early intervention strategies** to optimize outcomes.\n\n---\n### **5. Summary of Recommendations**\n| **Step**                          | **Action**                                                                 |\n|-----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **1. Confirm diagnosis**          | Genetic testing (CYP27A1) + clinical evaluation (DOCS 9 diagnostic criteria). |\n| **2. Cascade testing**            | Test siblings, parents, and consanguineous relatives for carrier status.     |\n| **3. Risk stratification**         | Use Hamming distance analyses (DOCS 2\u20133) to guide surveillance.            |\n| **4. Early intervention**         | Initiate CDCA therapy in affected relatives or prenatal screening for carriers.|\n| **5. Multidisciplinary care**      | Neurology, ophthalmology, and genetic counseling for long-term management. |\n\n---\n### **References**\n- **DOCS 1\u20133**: Hamming distance analyses demonstrating intra-familial variability.\n- **DOCS 4**: Role of CDCA in disease management.\n- **DOCS 6\u201310**: Diagnostic criteria, genetic testing, and clinical outcomes.",
