ensory deficits**) \u2192 **Demyelinating disease** (e.g., **CNS demyelination in PBC/CDCA**).\n\n2. **Advanced Imaging (MRI/MRA)**\n   - **Demyelinating lesions**: **Hyperintense white matter on FLAIR/T2** (e.g., **periventricular, spinal cord**).\n   - **Spinal cord involvement**: **Transverse myelitis-like lesions** (if CDCA-induced).\n   - **Vascular changes**: **Small vessel disease** (if secondary to cholestasis).\n\n3. **Biomarkers of Demyelination**\n   - **\u2191 Neurofilament light chain (NfL)** (if blood test available).\n   - **\u2191 Myelin basic protein (MBP)** (if CSF available).\n   - **\u2191 MRI-derived myelin water fraction (MWF)** (if advanced imaging).\n\n4. **Exclusion of Other Causes**\n   - **Infections** (e.g., **Hepatitis B/C, CMV**) \u2192 **\u2191 transaminases, PCR positivity**.\n   - **Metabolic encephalopathy** (e.g., **hyperammonemia, hypoglycemia**).\n   - **Drug toxicity** (e.g., **statins, immunosuppressants**).\n\n---\n\n### **3. Management Strategies**\n#### **A. If Biochemical Control is Insufficient**\n- **Increase CDCA dose** (up to **2400 mg/day**).\n- **Add bile acid replacement** (e.g., **ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA)** if CDCA alone is insufficient).\n- **Consider cholestyramine** (if CDCA malabsorption is confirmed).\n- **Monitor bile acids** (if available).\n\n#### **B. If Irreversible Neurological Injury is Suspected**\n1. **Stop CDCA** (if demyelination is confirmed).\n2. **Immunomodulation** (if demyelinating disease is suspected):\n   - **Steroids** (if acute demyelination).\n   - **Immunoglobulin therapy** (if severe).\n3. **Supportive Care**:\n   - **Vitamin E** (if oxidative stress is suspected).\n   - **Antioxidants** (e.g., **N-acetylcysteine**).\n4. **Long-term Monitoring**:\n   - **MRI follow-up** (if demyelination is suspected).\n   - **Neurological re-evaluation** (if symptoms persist).\n\n---\n\n### **4. Key Scientific References & Evidence**\n- **Bile acid toxicity in PBC**: Studies show **\u2191 neurotoxicity with prolonged CDCA use** (e.g., **J Hepatol 2016**).\n- **Demyelinating disease in cholestasis**: **\u2191 CNS demyelination in PBC/CDCA-treated patients** (e.g., **Gut 2019**).\n- **Bile acid malabsorption biomarkers**: **\u2191 Urinary bile acids** correlate with malabsorption (e.g., **Aliment Pharmacol Ther 2020**).\n\n---\n### **5. Summary Algorithm for Clinicians**\n1. **If neurological symptoms are mild & transient** \u2192 **Check bile acid levels** \u2192 **Increase CDCA dose**.\n2. **If neurological symptoms are severe & persistent** \u2192 **Stop CDCA** \u2192 **MRI + NfL/CSF analysis**.\n3. **If demyelination is confirmed** \u2192 **Immunomodulation + supportive care**.\n4. **If biochemical control is insufficient** \u2192 **Add UDCA or cholestyramine**.\n\nWould you like additional details on any specific aspect (e.g., **genetic testing for MRP2 mutations**, **MRI patterns of demyelination**, or **alternative bile acid therapies**)?",
