\n  - Educational support for parents to recognize early signs of disease progression (e.g., regression in developmental milestones).\n\n- **Potential Future Interventions:**\n  - **Enzyme Replacement Therapy (ERT):** Experimental approaches (e.g., *ASPA*-deficient enzyme delivery) are under investigation but not yet approved for CTX. Clinical trials should be monitored for emerging data.\n  - **Gene Therapy:** Preclinical studies in animal models have shown promise, but no approved therapies exist as of 2026.\n\n#### **Key Evidence from Literature:**\n- **No evidence suggests early intervention** is beneficial in asymptomatic children (DOC 3, 2023). However, **early detection enables proactive management** of potential complications (e.g., tendon xanthomas, cataracts).\n\n---\n\n### **2. Symptomatic Adult with Long-Standing CTX**\n#### **Key Considerations:**\n- **Disease Progression:**\n  - Adults with long-standing CTX (e.g., >10 years post-diagnosis) often present with **neurological decline**, including:\n    - **Pyramidal signs** (spasticity, weakness; **84.4% of patients in the Chinese cohort**).\n    - **Cognitive decline** (e.g., memory loss, executive dysfunction; **59.4%**).\n    - **Ataxia** (unsteady gait; **62.5%**).\n    - **Psychiatric disturbances** (e.g., depression, personality changes; **28.1%**).\n  - **Tendon xanthomas** (e.g., Achilles/patellar tendon swelling) and **cataracts** (e.g., bilateral juvenile-onset) are common systemic manifestations.\n\n#### **Recommended Priorities:**\n- **Neurological Stabilization:**\n  - **Physical Therapy:** To manage spasticity, gait impairment, and muscle atrophy.\n  - **Pharmacological Management:**\n    - **Spasticity:** Baclofen or botulinum toxin injections for focal muscle tightness.\n    - **Cognitive Support:** Behavioral therapy or cognitive rehabilitation for declining function.\n    - **Seizure Prevention:** If epileptic discharges are detected (e.g., via EEG), antiepileptic drugs (e.g., levetirac