ths) |\n| **Metabolic Surveillance** | Cholestanol levels, lysosomal enzyme activity | Cholestanol levels, bone density (DEXA)       |\n| **Systemic Management**    | None                                           | Tendon xanthomas, cataracts, osteoporosis     |\n| **Future Interventions**    | Monitor for early signs; enroll in trials     | Palliative care, symptom control              |\n\n---\n\n### **4. Key Differences in Approach**\n| **Aspect**               | **Asymptomatic Child**                          | **Symptomatic Adult**                          |\n|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| **Treatment Goal**       | Delay progression; prepare for future care    | Stabilize symptoms; manage complications      |\n| **Therapeutic Options**  | None (monitor only)                           | Symptomatic relief (ERT/gene therapy pending) |\n| **Prognosis**            | Variable; depends on early intervention        | Typically progressive; disability increases   |\n| **Family Impact**        | Genetic counseling; long-term planning        | Palliative care; psychosocial support         |\n\n---\n### **Conclusion**\n- **Asymptomatic children** should focus on **early detection, surveillance, and genetic counseling** without immediate intervention. **Neurological and metabolic monitoring** are critical to identify potential progression.\n- **Symptomatic adults** require **targeted management of neurological, systemic, and metabolic complications**, with **palliative care** as a priority for quality-of-life preservation. **Emerging therapies (ERT/gene therapy)** should be actively pursued in clinical trials.\n\n**Recommendation:** Collaborative care between neurologists, geneticists, and palliative specialists is essential to tailor treatment priorities to each patient\u2019s stage of disease.",
