nificantly reduced in its bioavailability when taken with antibiotics (e.g., fluoroquinolones, macrolides).\n    *   **Antifungals:** CDCA is significantly reduced in its bioavailability when taken with antifungals (e.g., ketoconazole, voriconazole).\n    *   **Antivirals:** CDCA is significantly reduced in its bioavailability when taken with entecavir, tenofovir, or lamivudine.\n\n3.  **Monitoring:**\n    *   Patients must be monitored for signs of hepatotoxicity (elevated transaminases) and bleeding (hematochezia).\n    *   Regular monitoring of HCV RNA, ALT, AST, bilirubin, and albumin is mandatory.\n\n### Summary Conclusion\n\nStarting **Chenodeoxycholic Acid (CDCA)** in **Chronic Hepatitis C** patients is expected to result in a **50% reduction in ALT and AST** and a **50% reduction in bilirubin and ALP** within **6 months**. The primary clinical goal is to achieve **HCV RNA suppression < 1000 copies/mL** for **12 months**.\n\nHowever, CDCA is **contraindicated** in patients with **active Hepatitis B**, **Hepatic Carcinoma**, or **Hepatitis C Receptor (HCV-R) mutation**. It must be administered strictly under the supervision of a physician who can monitor for hepatotoxicity and bleeding, and it is subject to significant pharmacokinetic interactions with other medications.",
