y inhibiting the uptake of bile acids, CDCA reduces the hepatic load of bile acids. Since bile acids are essential precursors for the mevalonate pathway, their reduced availability in the gut decreases the substrate available for CYP27A1.\n3.  **Induction of CYP27A1**: Chronic inhibition of the BSEP transporter leads to a compensatory upregulation of CYP27A1 expression in the liver. This upregulation increases the enzyme's catalytic efficiency, thereby enhancing the conversion of 15\u03b1-hydroxycholestan-3-one to the active intermediate and subsequently to cholesterol.\n4.  **Clinical Outcome**: The net result is a reduction in serum cholesterol levels and a decrease in the hepatic accumulation of cholestanol. This mechanism explains why CDCA is highly effective in reversing the metabolic defect caused by CYP27A1 deficiency.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nCYP27A1 deficiency results in the accumulation of cholestanol due to the inability of the enzyme to convert 15\u03b1-hydroxycholestan-3-one into the active intermediate required for cholesterol biosynthesis. CDCA treats this condition by inhibiting the BSEP transporter, which reduces bile acid load and induces the upregulation of CYP27A1, thereby restoring the metabolic pathway and lowering serum cholesterol.",
