d, transient diarrhea**, masking C. diff infection.  \n   - **Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)**:  \n     - **Functional gastrointestinal disorders** may mimic C. diff with **non-specific symptoms**, particularly in patients with **moderate to severe constipation**.  \n\n### 3. **Implications for Clinical Practice**  \n   - **Need for Differential Diagnosis**: Clinicians must consider **clinical context**, **toxin levels**, and **imaging** (e.g., **CT scans** for suspected C. diff colitis) to avoid misdiagnosis.  \n   - **Diagnostic Tools**: Integration of **toxin testing**, **PCR**, and **clinical evaluation** is critical for accurate identification of C. diff infection.  \n\n### Conclusion  \nThe underdiagnosis of C. diff is driven by **clinical ambiguity**, **limited diagnostic sensitivity**, and **confounding conditions**. Proper management requires **accurate diagnosis** through a combination of **clinical assessment**, **toxin testing**, and **advanced molecular techniques** to prevent mismanagement and prevent the spread of C. diff.",
